# {{{name}}}

A [quanthea](https://github.com/jboix/quanthea) plugin. It adds the connector kind
`{{{kind}}}`, "{{{displayName}}}", which speaks {{{text.title}}}.

## Develop

```sh
bun install
bun run verify     # Biome, tsc, the tests and the bundle
bun test           # the conformance suite and your tests
bun run build      # dist/plugin.js
```

`src/plugin.ts` holds the kind and its connection. The connection answers with empty results:
replace `test`, `describe`, `sampleValues` and `execute` with calls to your source, and the
settings and credential schemas with what it needs. The conformance suite in
`test/plugin.test.ts` runs the static checks quanthea runs at install and load; set its `live` to
`true` once CI has a source to query.

[`quanthea-plugin-sqlite`](https://github.com/jboix/quanthea/tree/main/examples/quanthea-plugin-sqlite)
is a complete plugin to compare with: a real source, the live checks and read-only safety.

## How a plugin works

- **One bundled ES module.** `bun run build` writes `dist/plugin.js` with everything the plugin
  needs. quanthea installs no dependency and runs no script of a plugin. Pure JavaScript only: a
  native module cannot work, since quanthea's image runs on amd64 and arm64.
- **The entry.** `src/plugin.ts` exports `kitVersion` and, as default, a function that receives
  the live kit and returns what the plugin adds: `{ connectors: [...] }`.
- **The kit is quanthea's own.** Build the schemas with `kit.z` and throw `kit.ConnectorError`.
  Import only types from `@quanthea/plugin-kit`: quanthea passes the live kit at load, so the
  bundle carries none of its code.
- **The manifest.** `package.json` holds the `quanthea` field and `"files": ["dist"]`, so the
  tarball holds the manifest and the bundle. Keep the `quanthea-plugin` keyword: it is how people
  find plugins on npm.
- **The query.** quanthea binds every variable before `execute` runs. Never build query text from
  a value.

[Publishing a plugin](https://github.com/jboix/quanthea/blob/main/docs/connectors.md#publishing-a-plugin)
in quanthea's docs covers each of these in detail.

## Try it in quanthea

```sh
bun pm pack
quanthea plugin install ./{{{tarball}}}
```

`quanthea plugin install` prints the pin to paste into the configuration file's `plugins.pins`.
Restart quanthea and add a connector of the kind "{{{displayName}}}".

In Docker, build a derived image:

```dockerfile
FROM ghcr.io/jboix/quanthea
COPY {{{tarball}}} /tmp/
RUN quanthea plugin install /tmp/{{{tarball}}}
```

## Publish

The Publish workflow publishes to npm when you publish a GitHub release whose tag is `v` and the
version in `package.json`, such as `v0.1.0`. It uses npm trusted publishing, which signs the
package with provenance and needs no token.

1. Publish the first version by hand: `npm login`, then `npm publish --access public`.
2. On npmjs.com, open the package's Settings, then Trusted publishing, and add GitHub Actions with
   your repository and the workflow `publish.yml`.
3. For each next version: set `version` in `package.json`, merge, then publish a GitHub release
   tagged `v<version>`.

People then install it with `quanthea plugin install {{{name}}}@<version>`.

## Kit versions

This plugin is built for kit version 0, the kit's beta, and depends on
`@quanthea/plugin-kit@{{{kit}}}`. While the kit is in 0.x, a minor version adds to it and a patch
version fixes it. npm's caret takes the fixes; moving to a new minor version is your choice, and
Dependabot opens a pull request for it. At 1.0, the kit's `kitVersion` becomes 1, and quanthea
stops loading plugins built for kit version 0.

